#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/id #uuid "3c82270a-33f2-53ee-8876-9d8c7cf8291b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21786" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228900 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/id #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6569" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228901 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/id #uuid "d01adef6-d3fb-5cd6-b778-5d44fb6702dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6572" :question/problem "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1bc3e8-11cc-570b-a70a-a1ec0620d5ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340582} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8fbd9a-4410-59c2-a62d-acf93aab0fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340583} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e1266b-774f-51ad-b38c-cc1ab5fb913d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340584} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b70014-fbb2-5647-8fa8-99e70d5c049f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2e1266b-774f-51ad-b38c-cc1ab5fb913d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340584} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bde4e4-2c80-5918-b0ab-dddf13e00a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40190b9a-c17d-51c6-81ac-4b525845a5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130076" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}-3x$ and $L({y}_{2})=x(x+1)$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3\\cdot x(x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3{x}^{2}+3x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3{x}^{2})+(3x-3x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aff340b0-9c42-5815-b12b-e25921cc6cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c739fe-67e3-5d99-9944-5247e970e0b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340589} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3cac3e-f6e4-589b-89ec-565c4b6a022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340590} {:answer/id #uuid "3a956442-dc1f-5cc2-8a44-555fd31a0ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340591} {:answer/id #uuid "39123b9c-2404-527a-b566-874d295d2927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3cac3e-f6e4-589b-89ec-565c4b6a022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340590} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3228f8d-eaf5-55d5-90e4-cfd1570f4374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "696173df-e20c-58f7-bff1-a24cad889831" :question/math-academy-id "q-130080" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232e60fa-8c23-5e48-ab1f-fa6c394a9b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+12x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340595} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cce0c3-a091-57d3-89e6-e7b4990d53a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340596} {:answer/id #uuid "3729e9dd-3307-50fd-a7b4-443ded46aada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340597} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8c5aec-1702-545b-9a58-69f9771b8bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340598} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6ab8b1-bb63-5617-8f69-ab77374e7cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0cce0c3-a091-57d3-89e6-e7b4990d53a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340596} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fb3393-8da0-58db-b8b8-c4543ae2c1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cb7c2e5-f581-57d0-8d98-1a27e90a0a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-130081" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=3x-5$ and $L({y}_{2})=(x-3)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3x-5)+(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6x-10+{x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+(-10+9) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53e427b-3abd-51ba-ae03-7545d111f98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340602} {:answer/id #uuid "bde60884-f006-5b9d-b9b7-15c2297dedeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340603} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6562f9-4f9c-523f-9e7e-385d5002ec8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340604} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa2ea62-6d54-54aa-a3e9-229771883181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340605} {:answer/id #uuid "fb102718-087b-5bc4-81ad-3b86d31838a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa2ea62-6d54-54aa-a3e9-229771883181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f546de6c-3e49-5599-a255-b30f098a94a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d8c3559-a0d3-57b0-8496-a5eb629e4fde" :question/math-academy-id "q-130261" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2ax{y}_{1}-2bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}]+b[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e12035-7a7a-5ba1-8c96-09db65f4ee36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340609} {:answer/id #uuid "b6032e5c-906c-5bf4-bada-bfd53badd310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340610} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e5b2bf-3132-5bed-b44c-086901eb8266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340611} {:answer/id #uuid "359614c8-439e-5414-bf7b-4aee41c2cfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340612} {:answer/id #uuid "2463731f-fd89-5d11-a2f5-35583b68f7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6032e5c-906c-5bf4-bada-bfd53badd310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340610} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4cd7ba-432e-5042-a7b9-98d32fcd0885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c09232e-7a55-5774-bf66-2b3b23800e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-130305" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+a{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}]+b[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{1}]+b[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b937f5-dbbe-5aa6-b2ab-e6fc6de00058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340616} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7afe22-4768-5a55-b0b4-735f4e16252d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340617} {:answer/id #uuid "c7506f22-779c-5612-ba66-443191b76f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7506f22-779c-5612-ba66-443191b76f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5f13fd-cfa9-55fc-a6f8-befbf797f379" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33497a63-2ad3-5b92-b633-c40b8613183d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbec9e-2eef-5a1a-82b5-6a44791ba5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbec9e-2eef-5a1a-82b5-6a44791ba5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340621} :answer-field/id #uuid "05778374-9485-5934-ab5b-f61a0567858f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027429b5-06bc-5e79-8ba3-e052a387b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad2825-8086-58ff-a468-d302df397153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340624} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7b78a4-14b1-5dc7-9652-585647659880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dad2825-8086-58ff-a468-d302df397153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340624} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc47255b-c513-5916-b1a0-c2568e81e177" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cddb1f-17fe-55b8-9ffe-2ace33ea67a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340627} {:answer/id #uuid "680da6a6-423a-5113-b16e-6a605081bb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680da6a6-423a-5113-b16e-6a605081bb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340628} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efa04f2-e13f-5f23-95e4-521ca4ffc591" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8878c6d8-f010-5cc2-ae57-e220015cf21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340630} {:answer/id #uuid "26401f57-20a2-525a-97d2-dae744007cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26401f57-20a2-525a-97d2-dae744007cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340631} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf0fd99-ed7f-512e-9d78-f4942425cd7a" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc0890d7-4776-52c7-95d6-0e2a44d1ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340633} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1bc30a-4092-5f24-b647-131c396dcbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1bc30a-4092-5f24-b647-131c396dcbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340634} :answer-field/id #uuid "049f029f-22b5-5205-9f87-6e94a9f62443" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0727572c-0559-503c-b0bd-d0aa08280f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-337728" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +2ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & +2ab\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d194bb28-8bbf-5b7b-813e-167aa0f64241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340637} {:answer/id #uuid "2d086215-3ee5-5fde-b490-82564ba43c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "569671fa-f39d-59df-b15d-852bcf0bbbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569671fa-f39d-59df-b15d-852bcf0bbbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a78f33-f8cf-557a-b259-52e4fa6b9f31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340636} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baebc494-16b5-51d5-88f6-ae0629bf805f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340641} {:answer/id #uuid "75b021e8-1b55-58c4-a5de-9e6e54d7ad46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b021e8-1b55-58c4-a5de-9e6e54d7ad46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340642} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f8c6fd-0294-5f99-bc94-8674893a24b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0cd5c28-76e2-58ac-9df3-9299b371727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340644} {:answer/id #uuid "372b4196-11a6-5da2-83d0-b80f8ab99fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{y}_{1}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0cd5c28-76e2-58ac-9df3-9299b371727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340644} :answer-field/id #uuid "a934b176-fec5-542b-a7af-29886a60ff09" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b088254f-98c0-5aa3-8098-3e8e7ba49363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340647} {:answer/id #uuid "29913555-7611-5394-9f5a-073bdeeae450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b088254f-98c0-5aa3-8098-3e8e7ba49363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340647} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b22b23-9242-5e16-83ec-018618d95300" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa9f0f1-cbd6-575b-864f-00ce62d8fbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340650} {:answer/id #uuid "b61e71d6-87a4-5d34-b9a8-6a426021580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61e71d6-87a4-5d34-b9a8-6a426021580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340651} :answer-field/id #uuid "73cf32e6-f5f7-5d34-ac5c-4dba8521ca1d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cae72f-0477-53ac-8fdb-0b2f5b762c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340653} {:answer/id #uuid "2da4177f-baf3-56e4-9e90-fe534f74ab68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da4177f-baf3-56e4-9e90-fe534f74ab68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ef1d87-7acc-5603-9d7d-2bf5675fd23f" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa9dd2f3-efc6-5afd-a9e5-5871df1e337b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337739" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2}+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & +a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot (2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{1}]+b[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0099fa-c260-5522-8349-b84319f3d9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340657} {:answer/id #uuid "9e00beba-7ccb-5dcd-a645-6ac3f82084ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} {:answer/id #uuid "314ee618-c0ac-565f-8046-5e30b17e6a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340659} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3d95e4-7576-55a1-aad6-1db486808546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "38375f01-3694-5244-ab9b-45e80378100b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e00beba-7ccb-5dcd-a645-6ac3f82084ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} :answer-field/id #uuid "118e46f1-be4c-5208-998c-6bd38711ed4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42f52e33-d273-52b4-89f9-837138729e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-337765" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640ca961-4afb-5b7b-9a10-0d3e6d27a4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340664} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee567f6-e9d8-52cc-a654-96427b7f9753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340665} {:answer/id #uuid "babac767-2c2b-5f88-a4d2-b339a17edf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340666} {:answer/id #uuid "7f28b1a6-0586-5da0-9655-ced3b94a3a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340667} {:answer/id #uuid "11a5e11f-338d-5e83-b87a-41aeb096fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11a5e11f-338d-5e83-b87a-41aeb096fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340668} :answer-field/id #uuid "52d93a9b-834e-59e5-a570-bee911d26b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "782599f5-3686-5999-a5ec-95d2e21b628e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337774" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d413f1-5023-5279-802c-c08d0e2013b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340671} {:answer/id #uuid "026cf490-5d92-5023-b356-8cb449b1a1f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340672} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae710d1-e402-5fbc-8063-58185b3831b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340673} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaaa7b3-ae57-5d6d-8e27-ee7172a90c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340674} {:answer/id #uuid "24772976-c4b1-5129-a3d4-b08c53202109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24772976-c4b1-5129-a3d4-b08c53202109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340675} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca36dd52-f70f-5f3b-b94f-956403d17746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "346bcac0-1447-50b0-bef6-e6f6a501ffe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-337777" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $xy,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a98b0d4-db57-58c0-8523-402735c11fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340678} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e136a9-f8b4-5f39-a1a1-b19aaec76d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340679} {:answer/id #uuid "cc815b64-636a-5055-9cf9-3a25a9493747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340680} {:answer/id #uuid "000ab1f1-7d5c-5a95-813b-e7fb90e63cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340681} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e40115-e366-5fd4-9457-afe4d83d5af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a98b0d4-db57-58c0-8523-402735c11fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340678} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ab82cd-1aa9-517a-9c8e-e69c0b654c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae970c05-8468-553f-965f-7819d89308c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337778" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3acd6d67-b727-518e-b03a-c63727d51748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340685} {:answer/id #uuid "6a49c2fb-acad-5e7d-a7f2-9f5e5c261a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340686} {:answer/id #uuid "668d26ca-9205-5010-a644-31a644074490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340687} {:answer/id #uuid "1292c7b1-f809-555d-a22f-9f83d1c06cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340688} {:answer/id #uuid "73b2bb90-c77c-5838-ad26-8497bcaf7d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "668d26ca-9205-5010-a644-31a644074490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340687} :answer-field/id #uuid "65a203b6-2565-590b-8ce9-8b4e023944f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b82a08be-c14d-5d29-a634-0558fbfaadff" :question/math-academy-id "q-338104" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d4f52f-fb16-50dd-b967-ddb1d1580aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} {:answer/id #uuid "8995a31d-bd20-5ffe-a31c-459c3295c4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340693} {:answer/id #uuid "2371e7fb-d44f-5656-b5ed-6ed804253cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340694} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0e5ad3-9790-5341-aafa-3f8153ac9a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340695} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7beb16-c2d7-50c0-900e-a455b82ed4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d4f52f-fb16-50dd-b967-ddb1d1580aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e0702c-75a2-5b2d-b62d-d9738c350e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e2de42e-6e49-52a4-9c7d-88b4b8bf3ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64900" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})={sin}^{2}⁡x+4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+4) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x+8 \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)+8 \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8631baaf-4da5-59ba-84ea-e88a9a9e7a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340699} {:answer/id #uuid "81411890-18b9-552d-864c-84393a589c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340700} {:answer/id #uuid "3eaccb6a-4bfe-5bb7-b050-bfc0eda6bfbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340701} {:answer/id #uuid "c39278fa-679b-5bd5-80c4-17833fe1db27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340702} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5826d-0ce2-5341-9649-84d0f39ea77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81411890-18b9-552d-864c-84393a589c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340700} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b72ec7-2bb9-5672-a6e3-42ce2071ee34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c63af792-171b-5ce8-938c-95e39526327b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65036" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+4$ and $L({y}_{2})=3(x-2)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-3(x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3{x}^{2}+12x-12 \\\\  & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ca37dd-f675-58c0-a7cb-25a4ed8d99c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340706} {:answer/id #uuid "846ac46d-8fff-5f8c-b26a-5aa8df04b65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340707} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a0e51d-63f2-5ad9-8ead-d55efbeb09e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340708} {:answer/id #uuid "90df5ac0-4c38-59aa-8d77-2a24d53e2b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340709} {:answer/id #uuid "811e4d5a-0614-5de5-b253-faf0eecf3eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "811e4d5a-0614-5de5-b253-faf0eecf3eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340710} :answer-field/id #uuid "f76c28be-4b0a-546d-9193-263aead3144e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25cb2680-651e-5a50-81aa-a03a420f17ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-79609" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \n$$"}]}
